{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LY4GzrECHuLeNrVFP88fpD","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — StructFuse\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main\\\\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (3)\\\\n\\\\n1. **True embedding-distance hard negative mining consistently improves contrastive code retrieval over random baselines, correcting the prior misconception that embedding negatives harm performance.** The reported \\\\\\\"harm\\\\\\\" (CoCoHaNeRe-derived, -27pp R@1) was an artifact of BM25-proxy negatives mislabeled as embedding-distance — genuine embedding mining yields +1.6–2.0pp R@1 across two architecturally distinct encoders (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B).\\\\n\\\\n2. **Structure-grounded (dataflow) hard negatives provide conditional additional benefit beyond embedding mining, mediated by encoder representation saturation.** When pretrained encoder representations are compressed (pos_sim > 0.98, as in Qwen), random and embedding negatives become too easy; dataflow-disconnected negatives force discrimination along a structural dimension the encoder cannot separate via text alone. When representation space is healthy (pos_sim ~ 0.80, as in StarCoder), embedding mining alone suffices. Mechanism: neg_difficulty margin analysis (+17% Qwen, +28% StarCoder) provides quantitative evidence for this interaction.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Scope-bounded inter-procedural data-flow analysis via CodeQL is a scalable, model-independent method for computing structural connectivity.** 0% timeout, 78% connected-not-gold rate (Z%), 123/131 repos with output at full scale. Method is a general-purpose structural negative generator applicable beyond code (molecular networks, circuit topology, KG retrieval).\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine\\\\n\\\\n- **Problem**: Repo-level code repair requires selecting relevant files from large codebases. Current retrievers rely on text similarity alone, but lexical similarity ≠ structural relevance. Hard negative mining is the standard approach to improve contrastive retrievers, yet the field lacks systematic understanding of what negative selection strategies work for code and why.\\\\n\\\\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**:\\\\n  - Text-based retrieval (SweRank, SWE-Fixer): blind to structural dependencies\\\\n  - Graph-guided at inference (LocAgent, RANGER, CodexGraph): structural graphs at inference only, no training signal\\\\n  - ARISE: data-flow but intra-procedural only (no call boundary crossing), runtime tool calls only\\\\n  - CoCoHaNeRe: reports embedding negatives harm code search — but uses BM25 proxy, not true embedding distance\\\\n  - Gap: no systematic study comparing negative mining strategies with proper controls, and no mechanistic explanation for when/why each strategy works\\\\n\\\\n- **Our contribution**: A systematic analysis of hard negative mining strategies for contrastive code retrieval. We (1) correct the misconception that embedding negatives harm performance (BM25 proxy ≠ embedding distance), (2) introduce structure-grounded negatives via inter-procedural dataflow and characterize when they provide additional benefit, and (3) provide mechanistic explanation via representation space saturation analysis.\\\\n\\\\n- **Headline result template**: \\\\\\\"True embedding-distance mining consistently improves code retrieval (+1.6–2.0pp R@1, 2 encoders), correcting prior reports of harm. Structure-grounded (dataflow) negatives provide further conditional benefit (+0.8–2.3pp) when encoder representations are saturated, with the interaction mechanism quantified via negative difficulty analysis.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Core Insight\\\\n\\\\nThe effectiveness of hard negative mining strategies depends on the encoder's representation geometry. When the pretrained space is saturated (high pos_sim → low margin between positive and negative), the encoder needs harder negatives from an orthogonal signal dimension (structure). When the space has healthy separation, embedding-distance mining alone captures sufficient difficulty gradient. This encoder-strategy interaction explains contradictory findings in prior work.\\\\n\\\\n## Dual-Pillar Motivation\\\\n\\\\n1. **Primary — Systematic analysis correcting misconceptions**: Prior work (CoCoHaNeRe) conflated BM25-proxy negatives with true embedding-distance negatives, leading to incorrect conclusions about hard negative mining for code. Our controlled comparison isolates the effect of each strategy.\\\\n2. **Secondary — Mechanistic explanation via representation geometry**: neg_difficulty analysis (margin distribution × strategy × encoder) provides explanatory depth beyond empirical comparison, enabling practitioners to predict which strategy suits their encoder without exhaustive experiments.\\\\n\\\\n## Formal Definition\\\\n\\\\nGiven retrieval domain D, structural graph G, similarity function s, query q:\\\\n**structure-grounded hard negative** = argmax_{x ∈ D : s(x,q) > τ ∧ ¬reachable_G(x,q)} s(x,q)\\\\n\\\\nThe structural graph is externally fixed and does not update with the encoder (drift-immune).\\\\n\\\\n## Title Options\\\\n\\\\n- Primary: \\\\\\\"What Makes Hard Negatives Work for Code Retrieval? A Systematic Study of Mining Strategies and Representation Geometry\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Alternative: \\\\\\\"StructFuse: When and Why Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives Improve Contrastive Code Retrieval\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Backup: \\\\\\\"Correcting Misconceptions in Hard Negative Mining for Code: Embedding Distance Helps, Structure Helps Conditionally\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit\\\\n\\\\n1. **EMNLP Main fit**: Empirical analysis + mechanistic insight papers are a strong EMNLP archetype (cf. \\\\\\\"Do Language Models...\\\\\\\" papers). Contribution is understanding, not just a new method.\\\\n2. **NLP audience**: Frame as contrastive learning methodology with code as instantiation. Representation geometry analysis generalizes beyond code.\\\\n3. **Against \\\\\\\"incremental\\\\\\\" criticism**: Paper corrects a misconception + provides mechanistic explanation + introduces new negative mining paradigm. Three contributions, not one marginal improvement.\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\\\\n\\\\n| Concern | Response | Evidence |\\\\n|---------|----------|----------|\\\\n| Only 2 encoders — can you generalize? | n=2 is minimum for interaction claim; we frame as observation with mechanism, not universal law; cross-architecture (decoder vs encoder-decoder) strengthens it | plan_013 (done), m2_full runs |\\\\n| Temperature regime specificity | Ablate τ ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2} | plan_014 |\\\\n| No downstream task evaluation | SWE-bench Lite end-to-end with LLM agent | plan_003 |\\\\n| Static vs dynamic mining confound | Clarify mining is static; add dynamic baseline | plan_015 |\\\\n| CodeQL scalability at larger scale | 0% timeout, 78% Z%, 123 repos; m1_reextract_v2 adds more | plan_006 (done) |\\\\n| Why not compare inference-time graph methods? | Complementary: they navigate at inference, we mine at training. Discuss relationship. | plan_017 |\\\\n| Single benchmark | Add RepoBench-R cross-file retrieval | plan_004 |\\\\n| Statistical significance | Bootstrap 95% CI for all comparisons; full M2 = 9 seeds × 3 strategies × 2 encoders | plan_016, m2_full |\\\\n| \\\\\\\"Complementary signal\\\\\\\" overstatement | Temper: \\\\\\\"conditionally beneficial\\\\\\\" not \\\\\\\"always complementary\\\\\\\" | Writing discipline |\\\\n| Missing related work | RepoFusion, GRACE, GraphCodeBERT, ANCE, curriculum HN mining | Writing task |\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence Status\\\\n\\\\n### Confirmed findings (basis for claims):\\\\n- D017: True embedding mining helps (+2.0pp Qwen, +1.6pp StarCoder) — corrects MVP BM25-proxy artifact\\\\n- D021/neg_difficulty: Qwen pos_sim=0.9855 (saturated), StarCoder pos_sim=0.7985 (healthy); margin differential explains strategy interaction\\\\n- m2_embed_verify (pilot): 3-strategy comparison confirms ranking (embedding > random, dataflow conditional)\\\\n- plan_013 (StarCoder pilot): Confirms cross-encoder pattern — embedding helps both, dataflow helps Qwen only\\\\n- plan_006: CodeQL scalability confirmed at 123-repo scale\\\\n\\\\n### In progress:\\\\n- m2_full_qwen: 9 seeds × 3 strategies (seed 1 running, best R@1=55.3% at step 1000)\\\\n- m2_full_starcoder: 9 seeds × 3 strategies (seed 1 running, R@1=45.4% at step 1000, still improving)\\\\n- m1_reextract_v2: expanded extraction for higher coverage\\\\n\\\\n### Planned (priority order):\\\\n- plan_003: SWE-bench Lite eval (biggest lever for paper strength)\\\\n- plan_014: Temperature ablation (Dream reviewer concern)\\\\n- plan_004: RepoBench-R second benchmark\\\\n- plan_015: Dynamic mining baseline\\\\n- plan_007/008/009: Additional ablations\\\\n\\\\n## Contingency (if full M2 results weaken claims)\\\\n\\\\n### All 18 runs confirm pilot pattern: Best case\\\\n- Full statistical power, paper as framed above\\\\n- Target: EMNLP Main\\\\n\\\\n### Dataflow benefit disappears even for Qwen at full scale: Pivot to 2-claim paper\\\\n- Drop Claim 2 to analysis observation\\\\n- Paper becomes: \\\\\\\"True embedding mining helps (correcting misconception) + CodeQL extraction is practical + representation geometry analysis explains prior contradictions\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Still publishable: correcting misconception + mechanistic analysis is sufficient for EMNLP Main\\\\n\\\\n### Embedding mining also loses advantage at full scale: Major reassessment needed\\\\n- Recheck data pipeline for bugs (full-scale data may have distribution differences)\\\\n- If confirmed: negative result paper — \\\\\\\"Why Hard Negative Mining Doesn't Scale for Code Retrieval\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Discuss with user before committing direction\\\\n\\\\n## Deprecated Claims (with reason)\\\\n\\\\n- ~~\\\\\\\"Embedding-distance hard negatives actively harm code retrieval\\\\\\\"~~ — FALSIFIED by D017. Original evidence (-27pp R@1) was from BM25-proxy negatives incorrectly labeled as embedding_distance in MVP. True embedding mining shows consistent improvement. Preserved here to prevent regression.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 9391, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01T2vGW8zkgWuF9gLa3rqyUw","session_id":"f4534d40-6470-414b-bd76-f15880fb0e07","uuid":"af80e195-5f96-4b9b-98d9-1ddeca25b83b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:31:43.336Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PQecYa7eKEoPSDZdSanwy4","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/structfuse-context/memory.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Inference-Time Graph Methods 文献对比\\\\n\\\\n## 对比表\\\\n\\\\n| Method | Paper | Venue | Graph Type | Usage Stage | Inter-proc Data-Flow | Task | Scalability | Key Diff from StructFuse |\\\\n|--------|-------|-------|-----------|-------------|---------------------|------|-------------|--------------------------|\\\\n| **CodexGraph** | Bridging LLMs and Code Repositories via Code Graph Databases | NAACL 2025 (arXiv:2408.03910) | Code graph DB (MODULE/CLASS/FUNCTION nodes; CONTAINS/INHERITS/USES edges) | Inference-time | No. Static analysis for symbol relations only | General repo-level coding (completion, issue resolution) | CrossCodeEval, SWE-bench, EvoCodeBench | Agent queries graph DB at inference; task-agnostic schema but no data-flow semantics |\\\\n| **LocAgent** | Graph-Guided LLM Agents for Code Localization | ACL 2025 (arXiv:2503.09089) | Directed heterogeneous graph (code entities + dependency edges) | Inference-time | No. Dependency edges, not data-flow | Fault localization | 92.7% file-level accuracy; SWE-bench +12% Pass@10 | Agent multi-hop graph reasoning at inference; needs fine-tuned 32B model |\\\\n| **RANGER** | Repository-Level Agent for Graph-Enhanced Retrieval | arXiv:2509.25257 (Sep 2025) | Knowledge graph + MCTS traversal | Inference-time | No | Code search, QA, cross-file retrieval, completion | CodeSearchNet, RepoQA, RepoBench, CrossCodeEval | MCTS-guided graph exploration; dual-stage (Cypher + MCTS); heavy inference cost |\\\\n| **RepoGraph** | Enhancing AI SE with Repository-level Code Graph | ICLR 2025 (arXiv:2410.14684) | Repo-level code graph (class hierarchy, call graph, file structure) | Inference-time | No. Structural relations only | Software engineering / issue resolution | SWE-bench (+32.8% relative); CrossCodeEval | Plug-in module for existing agents; repo-wide navigation; no semantic analysis |\\\\n| **GraphCoder** | Enhancing Repo-Level Code Completion via Code Context Graph | ASE 2024 (arXiv:2406.07003) | Code Context Graph (CFG + CDG + DDG) | Inference-time | Partial. Data dependence graph but **intra-procedural** only | Code completion | Repository-level benchmarks | Coarse-to-fine retrieval with CCG; closest to StructFuse in using data/control flow, but intra-proc and at inference-time |\\\\n| **CodeRAG** | Supportive Code Retrieval on Bigraph for Real-World Code Generation | arXiv:2504.10046 (Apr 2025) | Bigraph (requirement graph + DS-code graph) | Inference-time | No. Dependency + semantic edges | Repo-level code generation | DevEval (+40.90 Pass@1 w/ GPT-4o) | Dual-graph (requirements + code); agentic reasoning; high inference overhead |\\\\n| **ARISE** | Repository-level Graph for Agentic Fault Localization and Program Repair | arXiv:2605.03117 (May 2025) | Multi-granularity program graph (statement nodes + def-use edges) | Inference-time | Partial. **Intra-procedural** def-use edges + data-flow slicing tool | Fault localization + repair | SWE-bench Lite (300 issues); +17.0 Function Recall@1 | Closest to StructFuse in data-flow usage; but intra-proc only, agent queries at inference |\\\\n| **CodeGRAG** | Bridging NL and PL via Graphical RAG | arXiv:2405.02355 (2024) | Composed syntax graph (AST + DFG + CFG) | Both (GNN training + graph prompt at inference) | Partial. DFG extraction but function-level | Code generation (cross-lingual) | Multiple PLs | Only method with training-time component (GNN soft prompts); but graph also used at inference |\\\\n| **StructFuse** (Ours) | Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives via CodeQL Data-Flow | — | Inter-procedural data-flow graph (CodeQL taint tracking) | **Training-time only** | **Yes. Full inter-procedural** (source→sink across call boundaries) | Code retrieval (contrastive training) | Large-scale repos with CodeQL | Zero inference overhead; graph used offline for negative mining; inter-proc data-flow |\\\\n\\\\n## Discussion 要点\\\\n\\\\n### StructFuse vs Inference-Time Graph Methods: 互补性与差异性\\\\n\\\\n1. **Cost-accuracy tradeoff**: Inference-time methods (CodexGraph, RANGER, RepoGraph) achieve high precision by constructing and querying graphs per request, but incur significant latency (graph construction + traversal + LLM calls). StructFuse amortizes all graph computation to training time—once the encoder is trained with structure-grounded negatives, inference is a single embedding lookup with zero graph overhead. This makes StructFuse suitable for latency-sensitive production retrieval where inference-time graph methods are impractical.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Data-flow depth**: Most inference-time methods use structural graphs (call graphs, class hierarchies, file dependencies). Only GraphCoder and ARISE incorporate data/control flow, but limited to **intra-procedural** scope. StructFuse uniquely leverages **inter-procedural** data-flow via CodeQL's taint tracking, capturing cross-function semantic relationships (e.g., a sanitizer function that transforms data between source and sink across call boundaries). This deeper semantic signal generates harder, more informative negatives.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Complementarity**: StructFuse and inference-time methods are orthogonal and composable. A StructFuse-trained encoder can serve as the embedding backbone for systems like RANGER or CodexGraph, providing better initial retrieval that reduces the graph agent's search space. Conversely, inference-time graph methods can provide reranking on top of StructFuse retrieval for tasks requiring maximum precision.\\\\n\\\\n4. **Scalability model**: Inference-time methods scale linearly with query volume (each query triggers graph operations). StructFuse's cost is front-loaded: CodeQL analysis runs once per repository update, and the resulting negatives improve the encoder permanently. For high-QPS code search services, this one-time investment yields compounding returns.\\\\n\\\\n5. **Task generality vs specialization**: Inference-time graph agents (CodexGraph, RepoGraph) are task-agnostic but require powerful LLMs as backends. StructFuse produces a specialized retrieval encoder that excels at the code search task without LLM inference, making it deployable as a lightweight, efficient first-stage retriever in multi-stage pipelines.\\\\n\\\\n## Related Work 段落草稿 (~200 words)\\\\n\\\\n**Graph-Based Methods for Code Understanding.**\\\\nRecent work has explored leveraging code graphs to enhance LLM-based software engineering. Inference-time approaches construct and query graphs during each interaction: CodexGraph (Liu et al., NAACL 2025) builds a code graph database that LLM agents query via structured interfaces; RepoGraph (Ouyang et al., ICLR 2025) provides repository-level navigation as a plug-in module for SE agents; LocAgent (ACL 2025) and RANGER (2025) employ graph-guided multi-hop reasoning and MCTS-based traversal for code localization and retrieval, respectively. GraphCoder (ASE 2024) incorporates intra-procedural control-flow and data-dependence graphs for code completion retrieval, while ARISE (2025) extends this with statement-level def-use edges for fault localization. CodeGRAG (2024) bridges training and inference by using GNN-encoded graph prompts.\\\\n\\\\nThese methods achieve strong results but impose substantial inference-time costs—graph construction, traversal, and often multiple LLM calls per query. Moreover, their data-flow analysis remains intra-procedural. StructFuse takes a fundamentally different approach: we leverage CodeQL's inter-procedural taint tracking to generate structure-grounded hard negatives at **training time**, producing an encoder that captures cross-function data-flow semantics without any graph overhead at inference. This positions StructFuse as complementary to inference-time graph methods—it can serve as an efficient first-stage retriever whose embeddings already encode deep structural relationships that other methods must compute on-the-fly.\\\\n\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n## 参考文献\\\\n\\\\n- CodexGraph: arXiv:2408.03910, NAACL 2025\\\\n- LocAgent: arXiv:2503.09089, ACL 2025\\\\n- RANGER: arXiv:2509.25257, 2025\\\\n- RepoGraph: arXiv:2410.14684, ICLR 2025\\\\n- GraphCoder: arXiv:2406.07003, ASE 2024\\\\n- CodeRAG: arXiv:2504.10046, 2025\\\\n- ARISE: arXiv:2605.03117, 2025\\\\n- CodeGRAG: arXiv:2405.02355, 2024\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 8062, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QNTXiJZc3ypYERFAX1b5fX","session_id":"f4534d40-6470-414b-bd76-f15880fb0e07","uuid":"e16c6fb6-d8d3-4fe2-b721-45940dad7f2a","timestamp":"2026-05-14T08:31:42.956Z"}
